; BURST 1, not Blast 1, and the distinction is only visible in the code. Lancer.AOE_TYPE is
; {NONE, BLAST, LINE, CONE, BURST} (lancer.gd:96-100), so pattern = 4 is BURST.
;
; A BLAST HERE WOULD ALSO RESOLVE - corrected at the close, 2026-09-18, because the earlier reasoning
; here named a mechanism that does not exist. PassiveTerrainZone.get_voxels_to_apply_terrain calls
; UnitTile.get_voxels_from_pattern with no target tile (passive_terrain_zone.gd:13-14), so the
; parameter takes its Tile.INVALID default (unit_tile.gd:381) - and the very next line SUBSTITUTES the
; unit's own tile for it (:384) before anything reaches pattern.get_voxels (:397). The target is never
; INVALID, so `target_tiles.is_empty(): return` (range_pattern.gd:49) is not what BURST is dodging,
; and a BLAST would centre on the anchor perfectly well at any size.
;
; THE REAL DIFFERENCE IS THE FOOTPRINT, and it is why BURST is right anyway. AoeCalculator.blast
; iterates range(-radius, radius+1) around the given centre (aoe_calculator.gd:13-14); burst iterates
; range(-radius, center_size+radius) (:33-35) - i.e. the whole footprint plus the radius. The two
; agree only for a size-1 origin. BURST is the card's word, and it is the pattern that keeps covering
; the frame's own tiles if the Vauban ever grows to size 2, where a BLAST would quietly shrink to
; anchor +/- 1 and drop half of them.
;
; (mt_maar_terrain.tres two directories away declares a BLAST and is not disagreeing with this: a
; MARKER has no footprint to project from, so the distinction has nothing to bite on there.)
[sub_resource type="Resource" id="Resource_burst1"]
script = ExtResource("3_rangepattern")
pattern = 4
value = 1

; Difficult and nothing else. TerrainData merges by OR (terrain_data.gd:64), so a Maar under the
; field, or two Vaubans overlapping, is simply difficult ground once.
[sub_resource type="Resource" id="Resource_difficult"]
script = ExtResource("2_terraindata")
difficult_terrain = true

[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_gatedzone")
; THE GATE. PassiveGatedTerrainZone returns an empty voxel list - and therefore draws no ground fx
; either, since get_attached_fx walks the same list (passive_terrain_zone.gd:17-27) - until the
; owning gear's state says otherwise. It reads the key with gear.get_state(String(state_key), false),
; so this MUST spell action_grav_amped_interference.gd's FIELD_STATE_KEY exactly.
state_key = &"grav_amped"
terrain_data = SubResource("Resource_difficult")
range_pattern = SubResource("Resource_burst1")
; One voxel up, the Apocalypse crater's height (mt_apocalypse_difficult.tres). Left at 0 it would
; default to the pattern's value, which is the same 1 here - written out because "the same by
; accident" is not a thing to leave in a file.
height = 1
; SP3, design ruling 13: the same shared ground fx the Maar's own crater wears
; (mt_maar_terrain.tres). Gated exactly like the voxel list above - dark until state_key flips true,
; because get_attached_fx (passive_terrain_zone.gd:17-27) walks get_voxels_to_apply_terrain, which
; this class overrides to return [] while the gate is closed.
fxe = ExtResource("4_fxe")
